{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3265","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3265","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3265\",\"parent_revision\":\"bb23bbf8cb25d4acc940ab1706618144aa6c1af802747c1cdb17c44615ceb181\",\"root_revision\":\"bb23bbf8cb25d4acc940ab1706618144aa6c1af802747c1cdb17c44615ceb181\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Refactor\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-28\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\n## Story\\n\\nAs a maintainer of the `dh:impact-analyst` agent, I want its memory instructions to match the concise pattern every other `dh:` agent uses, so that this one agent's prompt isn't carrying ~140 lines of unrelated boilerplate.\\n\\n## Description\\n\\n`plugins/development-harness/agents/impact-analyst.md` has a `# Persistent Agent Memory` block (roughly lines 401-543) that is a full generic memory-system tutorial \u2014 covering user/feedback/project/reference memory types with examples entirely unrelated to this agent's actual job (e.g. Linear tickets, Grafana dashboards, \\\"user is a data scientist\\\"). It immediately follows \u2014 and duplicates \u2014 a concise, tailored memory instruction already given right before it (\\\"Update your agent memory as you discover codebase structure, module dependency patterns... Write concise notes about what you found and where\\\").\\n\\nEvery sibling agent that has a memory section (e.g. `alignment-analyst.md`, `rtica-assessor.md`) uses a 5-6 line version instead. This block is the single largest structural outlier found across all 30 agent files audited.\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\nThis agent's job is narrow (write an Impact Radius section). Carrying a generic memory-system tutorial on every invocation is pure token/context overhead with no behavioral payoff \u2014 the concise instruction immediately before it already fully specifies what to remember.\\n\\n## Observations\\n\\n- Related but different scope: #2998 covers *which* `dh:` agents have a memory directory at all (most grooming-pipeline agents have none) plus a confirmed split-brain duplicate memory-directory bug for `service-docs-maintainer`. This item is specifically about `impact-analyst.md`'s own prompt content being bloated and inconsistent with sibling agents' style \u2014 not about memory-directory naming or coverage gaps elsewhere.\\n- Flagged rather than fixed during a broader no-op/maintenance-note tightening pass: replacing the block needs a deliberately written short version matching the sibling-agent pattern, not a mechanical deletion.\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nThis agent's job is narrow (write an Impact Radius section). Carrying a generic memory-system tutorial on every invocation is pure token/context overhead with no behavioral payoff \u2014 the concise instruction immediately before it already fully specifies what to remember.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a maintainer of the `dh:impact-analyst` agent, I want its memory instructions to match the concise pattern every other `dh:` agent uses, so that this one agent's prompt isn't carrying ~140 lines of unrelated boilerplate.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Discovered during a no-op/maintenance-note sweep of development-harness skills and agents (2026-08-28)\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-28\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Observations\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- Related but different scope: #2998 covers *which* `dh:` agents have a memory directory at all (most grooming-pipeline agents have none) plus a confirmed split-brain duplicate memory-directory bug for `service-docs-maintainer`. This item is specifically about `impact-analyst.md`'s own prompt content being bloated and inconsistent with sibling agents' style \u2014 not about memory-directory naming or coverage gaps elsewhere.\\n- Flagged rather than fixed during a broader no-op/maintenance-note tightening pass: replacing the block needs a deliberately written short version matching the sibling-agent pattern, not a mechanical deletion.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"ca699109f51c9392bb1b4d10319cbbe752ac61e3adcc8b921a7c065f0bdf6ce4\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABRP60ug\"}"}